  "differences": [
    {
      "path": "$.coverage[16].reason",
      "old": "Both numerical differences, the original mixed expression b_(k+1)-b_n with independent indices, and a separate universal adjacent-difference argument are retained.",
      "new": "Both numerical differences, the original mixed expression b_(k+1)-b_n with independent indices, and the arithmeticity classification are retained in a complete result. The adjacent-difference justification remains internal; candidates with identical correct differences but opposite classifications require the learner to distinguish arbitrary pairs from consecutive terms."
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].intent.limitations[0]",
      "old": "Selecting a universal adjacent-difference argument does not establish independently producing the proof.",
      "new": "Recognizing the complete differences and arithmeticity result does not demonstrate independently deriving the symbolic subtraction or arithmeticity justification."
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].intent.task",
      "old": "Retain the printed mixed-index difference, both numerical differences and the universal adjacent-difference criterion.",
      "new": "Determine both numerical differences, the printed mixed-index expression, and the arithmeticity classification as one complete result."
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].intent.unit_reason",
      "old": "The general difference and adjacent specialization must be judged together to prevent conflating independent and equal indices.",
      "new": "The differences and classification form one whole result. Candidates with the same correct mixed expression but opposite classifications require distinguishing arbitrary term pairs from consecutive terms; no shared visible proof supplies the classification."
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].key.rationales[2].derivation",
      "old": "For the mixed expression subtract b_(k+1) from b_n instead: (2n+3)-[2(k+1)+3]=2(n-k-1).",
      "new": "All three differences are correctly computed. Incorrectly require every two-term difference to be constant: k=1,n=1 gives 2, while k=2,n=1 gives 4, so this mistaken criterion rejects arithmeticity. The actual criterion uses consecutive terms, whose difference is always 2."
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].key.rationales[2].misconception",
      "old": "Reverse the order only in the symbolic subtraction.",
      "new": "Demand a constant difference between arbitrary term pairs instead of a constant consecutive-term difference."
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].key.rationales[3].derivation",
      "old": "Replace b_(k+1) by 2k+3, then subtract 2n+3 to get 2(k-n).",
      "new": "Reverse every subtraction: b_1-b_2=5-7=-2, b_9-b_10=21-23=-2, and b_n-b_(k+1)=2(n-k-1). The same reversed adjacent subtraction b_n-b_(n+1) yields the claimed d=-2 instead of the actual d=2."
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].key.rationales[3].misconception",
      "old": "Ignore the +1 inside the first term's index.",
      "new": "Subtract the first named term from the second in every ordered difference, including the common-difference definition."
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].view.options[0].text",
      "old": "\u4e09\u4e2a\u5dee\u4f9d\u6b21\u4e3a $2,2,2$\uff1b\u4efb\u610f $n$ \u6709 $b_{n+1}-b_n=2$\uff0c\u662f\u7b49\u5dee\u6570\u5217\u3002",
      "new": "\u4e09\u4e2a\u5dee\u4f9d\u6b21\u4e3a $2,2,2$\uff1b\u662f\u7b49\u5dee\u6570\u5217\uff0c\u516c\u5dee\u4e3a $2$\u3002"
    },
    {
      "path": "$.questions[33].view.options[1].text",
      "old": "\u4e09\u4e2a\u5dee\u4f9d\u6b21\u4e3a $2,2,2(k+1-n)$\uff1b\u4efb\u610f $n$ \u6709 $b_{n+1}-b_n=2$\uff0c\u662f\u7b49\u5dee\u6570\u5217\u3002",
      "new": "\u4e09\u4e2a\u5dee\u4f9d\u6b21\u4e3a $2,2,2(k+1-n)$\uff1b\u662f\u7b49\u5dee\u6570\u5217\uff0c\u516c\u5dee\u4e3a $2$\u3002"
